<!-- Section 1: sec-intro -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Radius Calculator</h2>
  <p>A right triangle has two important circles attached to it: the incircle, which sits inside the triangle and touches all three sides, and the circumcircle, which sits around the triangle and passes through all three vertices. This calculator finds the radius of each circle, using a separate mode for each one.</p>
  <p>The inradius is <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{a + b - c}{2}]]')} />, where a and b are the legs and c is the hypotenuse. The circumradius is <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />, which needs only the hypotenuse. The formulas differ because they describe different circles.</p>
  <p>Take a 6-8-10 right triangle with <span set:html={rich('[[a = 6]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 8]]')} /> cm, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm. The inradius is <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{6 + 8 - 10}{2} = 2]]')} /> cm, and the circumradius is <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{10}{2} = 5]]')} /> cm. The incircle is a small circle of radius 2 cm tucked inside the triangle, while the circumcircle is a much larger circle of radius 5 cm passing through the vertices.</p>
  <p>The calculator keeps the two modes separate because they need different measurements: the inradius mode needs all three side lengths, while the circumradius mode needs only the hypotenuse. Pick the mode that matches the measurements you have.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: sec-calculate -->
<section id="sec-calculate" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Calculate the Inradius or Circumradius of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>This page offers two calculator modes. Mode 1 finds the inradius, the radius of the circle inside the triangle. Mode 2 finds the circumradius, the radius of the circle around the triangle.</p>
  <h3>Mode 1: Inradius Calculator</h3>
  <p>The inradius mode needs three inputs: leg a, leg b, and the hypotenuse c. It uses <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{a + b - c}{2}]]')} />. Choose it when you know all three side lengths and want the radius of the incircle.</p>
  <h3>Mode 2: Circumradius Calculator</h3>
  <p>The circumradius mode needs one input only: the hypotenuse c. It uses <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />. Choose it when you know the hypotenuse and want the radius of the circumcircle.</p>
  <p>Before you calculate, check that all measurements use the same unit, so convert first if they do not. Then select the mode, enter the measurements, click the Calculate button for that mode, and read the result in the unit you entered. Use Reset to clear the fields before a new problem.</p>
  <p>The page does not calculate angles, missing sides, area, or perimeter directly; the related calculators at the foot of the page handle those. If you only know the hypotenuse, you can find the circumradius, but not the inradius.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: sec-what-inradius -->
<section id="sec-what-inradius" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Inradius of a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>The inradius is the radius of the incircle, the largest circle that fits inside a triangle. It touches each of the three sides at exactly one point, and it is the distance from the circle's center to any side, measured at a right angle to the side.</p>
  <p>The center of the incircle is called the incenter, labeled I, the point where the three angle bisectors meet. Because it lies on every angle bisector, it is the same distance from all three sides, and that common distance is the inradius r, measured perpendicular to each side.</p>
  <p>In a right triangle, the incenter always lies inside the triangle, close to the right angle. For the 6-8-10 triangle, the inradius is <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{6 + 8 - 10}{2} = 2]]')} /> cm, so the incircle is a circle of radius 2 cm centered at I.</p>
  <p>A common error is to measure the inradius from the incenter to a vertex. That distance is longer than r and is not a radius of the incircle: the inradius always ends at a side, never at a vertex.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('radius-incircle.svg')} alt="Right triangle ABC with right angle at C, incircle inside touching all three sides, incenter I, and inradius r shown perpendicular to a side" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The incircle touches all three sides from inside, with center I and radius r = 2 cm in the 6-8-10 triangle.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: sec-what-circumradius -->
<section id="sec-what-circumradius" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Circumradius of a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>The circumradius is the radius of the circumcircle, the circle that passes through all three vertices of a triangle. Every vertex lies exactly on the circle, so the distance from the center to any vertex is the same, and that distance is the circumradius R.</p>
  <p>The center of the circumcircle is called the circumcenter, labeled O, the point where the perpendicular bisectors of the three sides meet. Lying on each perpendicular bisector makes O the same distance from all three vertices.</p>
  <p>A right triangle has a special circumcenter: it lies at the midpoint of the hypotenuse. The hypotenuse is the diameter of the circumcircle, so half of it is the circumradius: <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />. For the 6-8-10 triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{10}{2} = 5]]')} /> cm, and the circumcenter O sits at the midpoint of the 10 cm hypotenuse.</p>
  <p>The circumradius is measured from the circumcenter to any vertex, never to a side. In a right triangle, the right-angle vertex also lies on the circumcircle: the circle around the triangle passes through the corner with the right angle too.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('radius-circumcircle.svg')} alt="Right triangle ABC with right angle at C, circumcircle passing through all three vertices, circumcenter O at the midpoint of hypotenuse AB, and circumradius R" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The circumcircle passes through all three vertices, with center O at the midpoint of the hypotenuse and R = 5 cm.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: sec-difference -->
<section id="sec-difference" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Difference Between Inradius and Circumradius</h2>
  <p>The two radii describe two different circles. The incircle is squeezed inside the triangle and touches the sides, while the circumcircle wraps around the triangle and passes through the vertices. One circle is small and inside, the other is large and outside, and their centers sit in different places.</p>
  <p>The centers differ too. The incenter is the meeting point of the angle bisectors and lies inside the triangle, near the right angle. The circumcenter is the meeting point of the perpendicular bisectors and lies at the midpoint of the hypotenuse, on the edge of the triangle.</p>
  <p>The inputs differ too: the inradius needs all three sides, because the incircle is shaped by how the three sides close around it, while the circumradius needs only the hypotenuse, because the hypotenuse alone fixes the circumcircle as its diameter. In the 6-8-10 triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[r = 2]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[R = 5]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>The table below puts the comparison in one place. Read across any row to see how the same feature plays out for each circle, and use it as a quick check whenever a problem mentions a radius without saying which one it means.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Feature</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Inradius r</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Circumradius R</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Circle</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Incircle</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Circumcircle</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Position</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Inside the triangle</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Around the triangle</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Contact</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Tangent to all three sides</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Passes through all three vertices</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Center</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Incenter</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Circumcenter</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Formula</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">(a + b - c) / 2</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">c / 2</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Required inputs</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">a, b, c</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">c</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Measurement</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Center to a side</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Center to a vertex</td></tr>
