<!-- Section 6: sec-inradius-formula -->
<section id="sec-inradius-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Inradius Formula</h2>
  <p>The inradius formula for a right triangle is:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[r = \\frac{a + b - c}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here a is the first leg, b is the second leg, and c is the hypotenuse, the side opposite the right angle. The formula needs all three side lengths, because the incircle touches all three sides and its size depends on all of them. Leave one side out and the answer has no meaning.</p>
  <p>Work it step by step with the 6-8-10 triangle. Add the legs: <span set:html={rich('[[6 + 8 = 14]]')} />. Subtract the hypotenuse: <span set:html={rich('[[14 - 10 = 4]]')} />. Divide by 2: <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{4}{2} = 2]]')} /> cm. The result carries the same unit as the side lengths, centimeters here.</p>
  <p>The same value comes from an area route: <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{Area}{s}]]')} />, where <span set:html={rich('[[s = \\frac{a + b + c}{2}]]')} />. For the 6-8-10 triangle, the area is 24 cm² and the semiperimeter is 12 cm, so <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{24}{12} = 2]]')} /> cm. The <a href="/right-triangle-area-calculator/">right triangle area calculator</a> finds the area when you need it for this check.</p>
  <p>A third form, <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{ab}{a + b + c}]]')} />, is the same formula in different clothing. It is the area form with <span set:html={rich('[[Area = \\frac{ab}{2}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s = \\frac{a + b + c}{2}]]')} /> substituted in, and the proof section below shows how it simplifies back to <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{a + b - c}{2}]]')} />.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('radius-inradius-formula.svg')} alt="Right triangle with legs a and b and hypotenuse c labeled, showing the inradius formula r equals a plus b minus c over 2" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Inradius formula: r = (a + b - c) / 2, using all three side lengths of the triangle.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: sec-why-inradius -->
<section id="sec-why-inradius" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Does the Inradius Formula Work?</h2>
  <p>The proof starts at the incenter I. Because I is the same distance r from all three sides, the triangle can be split into three smaller triangles, each with I as one vertex and one side of the big triangle as its base.</p>
  <p>Each small triangle has height r, so its area is half its base times r. The three areas are <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{ra}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{rb}{2}]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{rc}{2}]]')} />. Adding them gives the area of the whole triangle:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[Area = \\frac{ra}{2} + \\frac{rb}{2} + \\frac{rc}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Factoring out r gives <span set:html={rich('[[Area = \\frac{r(a + b + c)}{2}]]')} />. Since the semiperimeter is <span set:html={rich('[[s = \\frac{a + b + c}{2}]]')} />, this becomes the neat relation:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[Area = r \\times s]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Rearranging gives <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{Area}{s}]]')} />. For a right triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[Area = \\frac{ab}{2}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{ab}{a + b + c}]]')} />. The Pythagorean theorem finishes the job: since <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[(a + b)^2 - c^2 = 2ab]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{ab}{a + b + c} = \\frac{a + b - c}{2}]]')} />. Both forms are the same fact, seen from two sides.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 8: sec-circumradius-formula -->
<section id="sec-circumradius-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Circumradius Formula</h2>
  <p>The circumradius formula for a right triangle is:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here R is the circumradius and c is the complete hypotenuse. The formula has only one input because the hypotenuse alone determines the circumcircle: it is the diameter of that circle, and a radius is half a diameter.</p>
  <p>The working is the shortest in this guide. With <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{10}{2} = 5]]')} /> cm. The circumcenter O sits at the midpoint of the hypotenuse, 5 cm from each vertex.</p>
  <p>The diameter form says the same thing in reverse: <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2R]]')} />. If you know the circumradius and need the hypotenuse, double it. A circumradius of 5 cm means a hypotenuse of 10 cm, and a circumradius of 6.5 cm means a hypotenuse of 13 cm.</p>
  <p>This shortcut belongs to right triangles only. In a triangle with no right angle, the circumradius depends on all three sides, so never carry <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} /> into one. The dedicated <a href="/right-triangle-circumradius-calculator/">right triangle circumradius calculator</a> covers this calculation in full detail.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('radius-circumradius-formula.svg')} alt="Right triangle with hypotenuse c shown as the diameter of the circumcircle, its midpoint O marked, and the formula R equals c over 2" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Circumradius formula: R = c / 2, with the hypotenuse as the diameter of the circumcircle.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 9: sec-why-diameter -->
<section id="sec-why-diameter" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Is the Hypotenuse the Diameter of the Circumcircle?</h2>
  <p>Take triangle ABC with the right angle at C, and let O be the midpoint of the hypotenuse AB. The claim is that O is the circumcenter, the same distance from A, B, and C. By construction, <span set:html={rich('[[AO = BO]]')} />, since O is the midpoint, so the only distance to check is CO.</p>
  <p>Draw the median from C to the midpoint O. In a right triangle this median is exactly half the hypotenuse, so <span set:html={rich('[[CO = \\frac{AB}{2}]]')} />, which makes <span set:html={rich('[[AO = BO = CO]]')} />. So O is equidistant from all three vertices and is the circumcenter.</p>
  <p>Since AB passes through O and both of its endpoints lie on the circumcircle, AB is a diameter of that circle. A diameter is twice a radius, so <span set:html={rich('[[AB = 2R]]')} />, which is the same as <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />. The hypotenuse earns its role as diameter from the right angle, not from its length alone.</p>
  <p>Thales' theorem states the same idea from the other direction: any angle inscribed in a semicircle is a right angle, so the hypotenuse of a right triangle must be the diameter of the circle through its vertices. The diagram below shows both centers at once: the incenter I inside the triangle and the circumcenter O at the midpoint of the hypotenuse.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('radius-centers.svg')} alt="Right triangle showing incenter I inside the triangle where angle bisectors meet and circumcenter O at the midpoint of the hypotenuse where perpendicular bisectors meet" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Two different centers: I is the meeting point of the angle bisectors, O is the meeting point of the perpendicular bisectors.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: sec-how-to-use -->
<section id="sec-how-to-use" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Radius Calculator</h2>
  <p>Follow the list for the mode you need. Both start with labeling the triangle correctly, because a swapped leg and hypotenuse is the most common source of a wrong answer.</p>
  <h3>Using the Inradius Mode</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify leg a, the first side forming the right angle.</li>
    <li>Identify leg b, the second side forming the right angle.</li>
    <li>Identify hypotenuse c, the side opposite the right angle and the longest side.</li>
    <li>Select the Inradius Calculator mode.</li>
    <li>Enter all three side lengths in the same unit.</li>
    <li>Select the unit that matches your measurements.</li>
    <li>Click Calculate Inradius r.</li>
    <li>Review the result and check that it is positive and smaller than either leg.</li>
    <li>Use Reset when starting another calculation.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Before you click Calculate, confirm the three lengths can form a right triangle: the hypotenuse must be longer than either leg, and <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2]]')} /> must equal <span set:html={rich('[[c^2]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Using the Circumradius Mode</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the hypotenuse c, the side opposite the right angle.</li>
    <li>Select the Circumradius Calculator mode.</li>
    <li>Enter c in the unit of your choice.</li>
    <li>Select the unit that matches your measurement.</li>
    <li>Click Calculate Circumradius R.</li>
    <li>Review the result, which should be exactly half the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li>Use Reset for a new calculation.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The circumradius mode needs only one measurement because the hypotenuse is the diameter of the circumcircle. Do not enter a leg here: it is shorter than the hypotenuse, so the result would be half the wrong length.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: sec-step-example -->
<section id="sec-step-example" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Example: Calculating Both Radii</h2>
  <p>Use the 6-8-10 right triangle with <span set:html={rich('[[a = 6]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 8]]')} /> cm, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm. The two parts below find each radius in turn, then compare the results.</p>
  <h3>Part 1: Calculate the Inradius</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Write the formula: <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{a + b - c}{2}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Substitute the values: <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{6 + 8 - 10}{2}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Add and subtract: <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{4}{2}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Divide: <span set:html={rich('[[r = 2]]')} /> cm.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The incircle has a radius of 2 cm. Its center I sits 2 cm from each side, and the circle touches the two legs and the hypotenuse from inside the triangle.</p>
  <h3>Part 2: Calculate the Circumradius</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Write the formula: <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Substitute the value: <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{10}{2}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Divide: <span set:html={rich('[[R = 5]]')} /> cm.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The circumcircle has a radius of 5 cm. Its center O is the midpoint of the hypotenuse, and the circle passes through all three vertices. Comparing the two results, the circumradius is two and a half times the inradius, which is normal: the circle around the triangle is much larger than the circle inside it.</p>
  <p>A quick cross-check: the area is 24 cm² and the semiperimeter is 12 cm, so <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{24}{12} = 2]]')} /> cm, matching Part 1, and <span set:html={rich('[[2R = 2 \\times 5 = 10]]')} /> cm, matching c.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('radius-complete-example.svg')} alt="6-8-10 right triangle with sides 6, 8, and 10 cm, inradius 2 cm, circumradius 5 cm, area 24 square cm, and semiperimeter 12 cm" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Complete 6-8-10 example: a = 6, b = 8, c = 10, r = 2, R = 5, area 24 cm², s = 12 cm.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: sec-examples-inradius -->
<section id="sec-examples-inradius" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Worked Examples of Calculating Inradius</h2>
  <p>Each example states the given values, the substitution, and the final answer, and every example keeps its units consistent from start to finish. All six triangles are valid right triangles, checked with the Pythagorean theorem.</p>
  <h3>Example 1: Basic 3-4-5 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 3]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 4]]')} /> cm, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 5]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{3 + 4 - 5}{2} = \\frac{2}{2} = 1]]')} /> cm. Check: <span set:html={rich('[[3^2 + 4^2 = 9 + 16 = 25 = 5^2]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Example 2: 5-12-13 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 12]]')} /> cm, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 13]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{5 + 12 - 13}{2} = \\frac{4}{2} = 2]]')} /> cm. Check: <span set:html={rich('[[5^2 + 12^2 = 25 + 144 = 169 = 13^2]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Example 3: 8-15-17 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 8]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 15]]')} /> cm, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 17]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{8 + 15 - 17}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3]]')} /> cm. Check: <span set:html={rich('[[8^2 + 15^2 = 64 + 225 = 289 = 17^2]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Example 4: Decimal Measurements</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 7.5]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 10]]')} /> cm, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 12.5]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{7.5 + 10 - 12.5}{2} = \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5]]')} /> cm. This is the 6-8-10 triangle scaled by 1.25, so its inradius scales the same way. Check: <span set:html={rich('[[7.5^2 + 10^2 = 56.25 + 100 = 156.25 = 12.5^2]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Example 5: Larger Measurements</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 9]]')} /> m, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 12]]')} /> m, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 15]]')} /> m, <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{9 + 12 - 15}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3]]')} /> m. This is the 3-4-5 triangle scaled by 3. Check: <span set:html={rich('[[9^2 + 12^2 = 81 + 144 = 225 = 15^2]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Example 6: Different Units</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 0.09]]')} /> m, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 0.12]]')} /> m, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 0.15]]')} /> m, convert to centimeters first: 9 cm, 12 cm, and 15 cm. Then <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{9 + 12 - 15}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3]]')} /> cm. Check: <span set:html={rich('[[9^2 + 12^2 = 81 + 144 = 225 = 15^2]]')} />.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: sec-examples-circumradius -->
<section id="sec-examples-circumradius" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Worked Examples of Calculating Circumradius</h2>
  <p>Each circumradius example needs only the hypotenuse, so each one is a single division by 2. The variety comes from the units and the size of the numbers, not from the method, which never changes.</p>
  <h3>Example 1: Hypotenuse 10 cm</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{10}{2} = 5]]')} /> cm. This is the circumradius of the 6-8-10 triangle.</p>
  <h3>Example 2: Hypotenuse 13 cm</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 13]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{13}{2} = 6.5]]')} /> cm. This is the circumradius of the 5-12-13 triangle.</p>
  <h3>Example 3: Hypotenuse 24 m</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 24]]')} /> m, <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{24}{2} = 12]]')} /> m.</p>
  <h3>Example 4: Hypotenuse 7.5 cm</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 7.5]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{7.5}{2} = 3.75]]')} /> cm. The diameter check runs in reverse: <span set:html={rich('[[2 \\times 3.75 = 7.5]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <h3>Example 5: Hypotenuse 180 mm</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 180]]')} /> mm, <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{180}{2} = 90]]')} /> mm, which is 9 cm.</p>
  <h3>Example 6: Hypotenuse 1.2 m</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 1.2]]')} /> m, <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{1.2}{2} = 0.6]]')} /> m, which is 60 cm.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: sec-validity -->
<section id="sec-validity" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Right Triangle Measurements Are Valid</h2>
  <p>A radius answer is only as good as the triangle behind it, so check the measurements before you calculate. For a right triangle, the sides must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The hypotenuse must also be the longest side, so <span set:html={rich('[[c > a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c > b]]')} />. A leg that is longer than the hypotenuse cannot belong to any right triangle, and a hypotenuse shorter than a leg means the sides were mislabeled.</p>
  <p>Check the 3-4-5 triangle: <span set:html={rich('[[3^2 + 4^2 = 9 + 16 = 25 = 5^2]]')} />, so the sides agree, and the inradius comes out positive: <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{3 + 4 - 5}{2} = 1]]')} /> cm. A valid nondegenerate right triangle always gives a positive inradius, because the legs together are longer than the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p>Invalid measurements betray themselves in the inradius formula. If the legs add up to less than the hypotenuse, r comes out negative, which describes no real circle, and if they add up to exactly the hypotenuse, r is zero and the triangle is flat. Either result means the inputs are wrong. The <a href="/right-triangle-side-calculator/">right triangle side calculator</a> can rebuild a missing side from valid measurements.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 15: sec-area-relation -->
<section id="sec-area-relation" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between Inradius, Circumradius, and Triangle Area</h2>
  <p>The inradius, the area, and the semiperimeter of a triangle are tied together by one relation, and it works for any triangle, not just right triangles:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[Area = r \\times s]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The area measures how much surface the triangle covers, the semiperimeter measures half its boundary, and their ratio is the radius of the circle that fits inside.</p>
  <p>For a right triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[Area = \\frac{ab}{2}]]')} />, since the two legs are the base and the height, and the circumradius joins through <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2R]]')} />. In the 6-8-10 triangle, the area is 24 cm², the semiperimeter is 12 cm, <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{24}{12} = 2]]')} /> cm, and <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{10}{2} = 5]]')} /> cm. The altitude to the hypotenuse fits the same pattern: <span set:html={rich('[[h = \\frac{ab}{c}]]')} />, which gives <span set:html={rich('[[h = \\frac{48}{10} = 4.8]]')} /> cm here.</p>
  <p>The two radii describe different aspects of the same triangle: the inradius depends on the area and the perimeter together, while the circumradius depends on the hypotenuse alone. Neither one can be found from the area by itself.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 16: sec-perimeter-relation -->
<section id="sec-perimeter-relation" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between Inradius and Perimeter</h2>
  <p>The perimeter of a right triangle is <span set:html={rich('[[P = a + b + c]]')} />, and the semiperimeter is half of that, <span set:html={rich('[[s = \\frac{a + b + c}{2}]]')} />. The inradius can be written as <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{Area}{s}]]')} />, so for a fixed area, a larger semiperimeter means a smaller inradius.</p>
  <p>Use the 6-8-10 triangle: the perimeter is 24 cm, the semiperimeter is 12 cm, and with an area of 24 cm², <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{24}{12} = 2]]')} /> cm. Compare the 3-4-5 triangle: its perimeter is 12 cm, its semiperimeter is 6 cm, its area is 6 cm², and <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{6}{6} = 1]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>The perimeter alone cannot determine the inradius: two triangles can share a perimeter of 24 cm and have different areas and different inradii. Do not confuse the semiperimeter with the perimeter: using P where s belongs would halve the answer. The <a href="/right-triangle-perimeter-semiperimeter-calculator/">right triangle perimeter and semiperimeter calculator</a> finds both values when you need them.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 17: sec-properties -->
<section id="sec-properties" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Important Properties of Right Triangle Radii</h2>
  <p>Nine properties follow directly from the two formulas. Each one doubles as a sense check on a new answer.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The inradius is always positive in a valid nondegenerate triangle, because the two legs together are longer than the hypotenuse, so <span set:html={rich('[[a + b - c > 0]]')} />.</li>
    <li>The incenter lies inside the triangle, at the meeting point of the three angle bisectors.</li>
    <li>The inradius is perpendicular to each side at the tangency point, since a radius to a tangent meets it at a right angle.</li>
    <li>The circumcenter lies at the midpoint of the hypotenuse in a right triangle, and nowhere else.</li>
    <li>The circumradius is exactly half the hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />, in every right triangle.</li>
    <li>The circumcircle passes through all three vertices, including the right-angle vertex.</li>
    <li>The incircle is tangent to all three sides and touches each side at exactly one point.</li>
    <li>The circumradius is greater than the inradius for a nondegenerate right triangle, so <span set:html={rich('[[R > r]]')} /> always holds here.</li>
    <li>The two radius calculations require different input measurements: the inradius needs all three sides, while the circumradius needs only the hypotenuse.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The last property is the one students forget most often: when a problem gives only the hypotenuse, the circumradius is within reach and the inradius is not.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 18: sec-mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Inradius and Circumradius</h2>
  <p>Each mistake below is followed by its correction. Most trace back to mixing up the two circles, not to arithmetic.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Confusing inradius with circumradius: r belongs to the circle inside the triangle, R to the circle around it. In the 6-8-10 triangle, r is 2 cm and R is 5 cm.</li>
    <li>Using <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} /> to calculate the inradius: halving the hypotenuse gives the circumradius, not r.</li>
    <li>Using <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{a + b - c}{2}]]')} /> to calculate the circumradius: it gives a smaller number that has nothing to do with the circle through the vertices.</li>
    <li>Entering a leg instead of the hypotenuse in circumradius mode: a leg is shorter than the hypotenuse, so halving it gives half the wrong length.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to divide by two in the inradius formula: <span set:html={rich('[[a + b - c]]')} /> alone is twice the inradius. In the 6-8-10 triangle, the sum is 4, but r is 2.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to divide by two in the circumradius formula: the full hypotenuse is the diameter, not the radius. With c = 10 cm, R is 5 cm, not 10 cm.</li>
    <li>Entering an invalid triangle: if <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2]]')} /> does not equal <span set:html={rich('[[c^2]]')} />, the sides form no right triangle.</li>
    <li>Mixing units: centimeters for one side and meters for another give a meaningless subtraction. Convert everything to one unit first.</li>
    <li>Confusing the incenter with the circumcenter: the incenter is inside the triangle, the circumcenter is at the midpoint of the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li>Assuming the incircle passes through the vertices: the incircle touches the sides, and its points never reach a vertex.</li>
    <li>Assuming the circumcircle touches all three sides: the circumcircle passes through the vertices, and it touches no side along its length.</li>
    <li>Rounding too early: keep full precision until the last step, since rounding an intermediate value shifts the answer.</li>
    <li>Confusing radius with diameter: the diameter is twice the radius, so a circumradius of 5 cm means a diameter of 10 cm, which is the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li>Using the wrong calculator mode: the inradius mode needs three inputs and the circumradius mode needs one. Pick the mode that matches the measurements you have.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If an answer looks wrong, check the mode first, then the labels, then the units. Most of the errors above vanish once the right mode is selected and the hypotenuse is correctly identified.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 19: sec-applications -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practical Applications of Triangle Radii</h2>
  <p>The most direct use is geometry homework and classroom work, where radius problems test whether students can tell the incircle from the circumcircle and pick the right formula for each. Teachers use the 6-8-10 triangle because both radii come out as whole numbers.</p>
  <p>Circle construction is a second use: drawing an incircle needs the incenter and the inradius, while drawing a circumcircle needs the circumcenter and the circumradius. Technical drawing uses the same ideas whenever a triangular shape must be fitted with a circular hole or set inside a circular frame.</p>
  <p>Mathematical proofs use both radii as stepping stones: <span set:html={rich('[[Area = rs]]')} /> connects area, perimeter, and inradius in one line, and Thales' theorem connects the right angle with the diameter of the circumcircle. Geometric modeling uses the same relations to work out clearances, such as how large a circular part must be to enclose a triangular piece.</p>
  <p>One condition limits every application: these formulas describe ideal triangles and circles, not approximate field measurements, so practical design work may need additional tolerances beyond the radius alone.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 20: sec-practice -->
<section id="sec-practice" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practice Problems With Answers</h2>
  <p>Try all six problems on paper before reading the answers. None of the values repeat the worked examples, and every triangle is valid.</p>
  <h3>Problem 1: Find the Inradius</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has legs <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[b = 12]]')} /> cm and hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c = 13]]')} /> cm. Find the inradius.</p>
  <h3>Problem 2: Find the Circumradius</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c = 26]]')} /> cm. Find the circumradius.</p>
  <h3>Problem 3: Calculate Both Radii</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has <span set:html={rich('[[a = 9]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 40]]')} /> cm, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 41]]')} /> cm. Find both the inradius and the circumradius.</p>
  <h3>Problem 4: Verify Triangle Validity</h3>
  <p>A triangle is said to have sides 7 cm, 24 cm, and 25 cm. Check with the Pythagorean theorem that it is a right triangle, then find its inradius.</p>
  <h3>Problem 5: Inradius From Area and Semiperimeter</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has legs 8 cm and 15 cm. Find its area and semiperimeter, then use <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{Area}{s}]]')} /> to find the inradius.</p>
  <h3>Problem 6: Identify the Correct Mode</h3>
  <p>You know only that the hypotenuse of a right triangle is 18 cm. Which calculator mode can you use, which radius can you find, and what is its value? Explain why the other radius cannot be found from this information alone.</p>
  <h3>Practice Problem Answers</h3>
  <p>Answer 1: <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{5 + 12 - 13}{2} = \\frac{4}{2} = 2]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 2: <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{26}{2} = 13]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 3: <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{9 + 40 - 41}{2} = \\frac{8}{2} = 4]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{41}{2} = 20.5]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 4: <span set:html={rich('[[7^2 + 24^2 = 49 + 576 = 625 = 25^2]]')} />, so it is a right triangle, and <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{7 + 24 - 25}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 5: <span set:html={rich('[[Area = \\frac{8 \\times 15}{2} = 60]]')} /> cm², <span set:html={rich('[[c = 17]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[s = \\frac{8 + 15 + 17}{2} = 20]]')} /> cm, so <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{60}{20} = 3]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 6: Use the circumradius mode: <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{18}{2} = 9]]')} /> cm. The inradius cannot be found, because <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{a + b - c}{2}]]')} /> needs both legs, and many right triangles share a hypotenuse of 18 cm.</p>
</section>

